# Module Entitlement UX

Frontend module checks are for navigation and user experience.

Backend middleware remains the security authority.

Do not treat frontend module gating as sufficient authorization.

## Dual registration

When adding a tenant feature that belongs to a sellable module, update both:

1. `lib/tenant-modules.js`
   - path-to-module rules
   - used by `ModuleRouteGuard`

2. `components/shell/sidebar/main-nav.js`
   - navigation item module metadata
   - used by sidebar filtering

These two mappings must remain aligned.

Updating only one creates inconsistent navigation or route behavior.

When adding a navigable feature, also add:

- The route constant under ROUTES in components/shell/sidebar/main-nav.js.
- The actual sidebar navigation item in buildMainNavItems().
- The correct module metadata on that navigation item.

## Module codes

Use backend module codes exactly.

Examples include:

- `core`
- `master_data`
- `inventory`
- `sales`
- `purchasing`

Do not invent frontend-only module names.

## Missing-module behavior

Follow the existing UX:

- notify the user
- redirect to the overview page
- do not display inaccessible module pages

The missing-module experience is currently handled primarily by `ModuleRouteGuard` and shell messaging.

Do not rely on a localized `MODULE_NOT_ENTITLED` API error key unless it is actually added to the translation catalog.

## Permissions

Action-level frontend permission gating is not currently implemented.

Do not invent a `can()` architecture as part of an unrelated feature.

Backend RBAC remains authoritative.

Any future frontend permission system should be introduced as a coordinated feature.